# Spectra — Game state

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
The build owns the shape of the game's state entirely.

The one contract fixed over the state is the debugging and automation surface
specified for this build. Every operation that surface carries is a read or a pose
of the running game, so the state holds what those operations read, honors what they
write, and returns to its title-screen values when the surface's `reset` is called.
Beyond satisfying that surface, how the state is organized is yours to decide.

Whatever its shape, the state carries the following.

## The screen and the run

- The screen the game is showing, one of `title`, `howto`, `stageIntro`, `inWave`,
  `paused`, `stageCleared`, and `gameOver`.
- The sub-phase of the `inWave` screen, `live` or `ready`, and the seconds left in
  whichever hold the current screen or phase is running.
- The item highlighted on whichever vertical menu the current screen shows, counted
  from `0`.
- The score, the lives remaining, and the stage being played.
- Whether the run's one extra life has already been paid.
- How many of the current challenge stage's drones have been destroyed.

## The band systems

- The resonance meter, from `0` to `RESONANCE_MAX`.
- The seconds of spectral inversion remaining, `0` for none.

## The field

- The ship: the center `x` of its lane position, its band, the seconds of fire
  lockout left, the seconds until it may fire again, and whether its contact test is
  running.
- The live discharge wave: whether one is running, and its current radius.
- Every drone on the field, each with a distinct id, its kind, its center, its
  stored band, its phase, the seconds it has been in that phase, the center of its
  formation slot, the entry group it arrives with, a Flux's band clock, whether a
  Prism's shell stands, how many shots it has taken during its current dive, and the
  three faculty gates the debug surface poses.
{{#if (eq variant.slug "overload")}}
  Each drone also carries its charge, a whole number from `0` to `OVERLOAD_AT`.
{{/if}}
- Every bullet in flight, each with a distinct id, its center, its velocity, its
  band, and whether it is the player's.
- Every drone-burst playing, each with a distinct id, its center, the footprint it
  is played at, the seconds it has been playing, and its own particle simulation.

## The wave's own schedules

- Whether the wave's entry of drones is running, and whether the assault's dive
  launching is running. Both are on when a run begins.
- The wave's entry clock, which its entry groups are released against, and the
  wave's dive clock together with the gap the next dive launch waits for.

## The rest

- The simulation time, in seconds, which every update adds its delta to whatever the
  screen.
- Whether sound is muted, refreshed from the runtime's mute bit in every update.
- The state of the seeded random generator, which the wave's layout, the choice of
  which drone dives next, the gap before the next dive, a Flux's starting phase, and
  each burst's scatter are all drawn from.
- Whatever the game needs to hand a fresh id to the next drone, bullet, or burst it
  creates, so no two entities live at one moment share an id.

A drone's effective band, whether a Flux is shimmering, whether a discharge is
ready, whether the stage is a challenge stage, whether the ship is alive, and the
four stage-scaled figures are all derived by the formulas the other specs give,
rather than stored.
{{else if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
`src/game.ts` declares and exports the state the game is written against. This
section is that declaration: write it into `src/game.ts` exactly as it stands here.
The state is a value the engine holds and every frame replaces, so every field and
every array is declared `readonly`: a frame builds the next state from the current
one rather than writing into it, and every reader is handed the state as
`DeepReadonly<SpectraState>` in any case.

`ParticleSimulator` comes from `@test-cabinet/particle-runtime`, the runtime
`specs/assets.md` states the drone-burst is played with.

