{{else}}
`src/game.ts` declares and exports the state the game is written against. This
section is that declaration: write it into `src/game.ts` exactly as it stands here.
The state is the world's game state: a class extending the engine's `GameState`,
named by the game mode as its `gameStateClass`, so the engine builds one instance of
it when the world opens and `engine.world.state` is that instance. Wherever this
specification writes `state.foo`, it means a field of that instance.

The game runs in one world for the whole session. The game definition registers the
single level `LEVELS` names, the game never opens another, and every screen is a
value of `screen`, so the state below is built once and lives until the page closes.
The actors the field carries are tagged with the names in `TAGS`, one per actor
kind, so the ship, the drones, the two bullet kinds, and the bursts are each found by
tag. The framework's
states are live objects: a tick writes the fields it advances in place, and each
field initializer below is that field's title-screen value, the same value the debug
surface's `reset` restores.

`GameState` comes from the engine's package, `ParticleSimulator` from
`@test-cabinet/particle-runtime`, and `DEFAULT_SEED`, `DIVE_FIRST_DELAY`, and
`START_LIVES` from `src/constants.ts`.

