## The screen and the run

- `screen` — the screen currently shown, one of the seven `specs/ui.md` defines.
- `phase` — the sub-phase of the `inWave` screen, as `specs/progression.md` defines
  it. It holds `live` on every other screen.
- `phaseTimer` — the seconds left in whichever hold the current screen or phase is
  running, counting down against each update's delta and resting at `0`.
- `menuIndex` — the highlighted item on whichever vertical menu `screen` is showing,
  counted from `0`. It rests at `0` on `inWave`.
- `score`, `lives`, `stage` — the run's figures.
- `extraLifeAwarded` — whether the run's one extra life has been paid, as
  `specs/progression.md` states.
- `challengeHits` — how many of the current challenge stage's drones the run has
  destroyed. It returns to `0` when a challenge stage's wave is built.

## The band systems

- `resonance` — the meter, from `0` to `RESONANCE_MAX`.
- `inversion` — the seconds of spectral inversion left, `0` for none.

## `ShipState` and `DischargeState`

- The ship carries `x`, the center of its lane position; `band`, the band it holds;
  `lockout`, the seconds of post-flip fire lockout left; `cooldown`, the seconds
  until it may fire again; and `contact`, whether its contact test runs, which the
  debug surface poses.
- The discharge carries `active`, whether a wave is running, and `radius`, that
  wave's current radius in logical units. Both rest at `false` and `0`.

## `DroneState`

One drone on the field.

- `id` — distinct among every entity live at that moment, and kept for as long as
  the drone exists.
- `kind` — `"shard"`, `"flux"`, or `"prism"`.
- `x`, `y` — the drone's center.
- `band` — its stored band. On a Prism this is the shell's band, and the core's is
  the opposite.
- `phase` — one of the four phases `specs/swarm.md` defines.
- `phaseClock` — the seconds the drone has been in its current phase. It returns to
  `0` whenever the phase changes, however the change came about, so the path a
  phase runs starts from its beginning.
- `slotX`, `slotY` — the center of the drone's resting formation slot. A challenge
  drone never settles, and its slot holds the point it entered at.
- `entryGroup` — the entry group the drone arrives with, counted from `0`, as
  `specs/swarm.md` states.
- `bandClock` — a Flux's position inside its current band window, in seconds. It
  rests at `0` on the other two kinds.
- `shellAlive` — whether a Prism's outer shell stands. It is `true` on the other two
  kinds.
- `shotsFired` — how many shots the drone has taken during its current dive. It
  returns to `0` with `phaseClock`.
- `travel`, `oscillation`, `fire` — the three faculty gates the debug surface poses.
  Each is `true` for a drone the game itself brought in.
{{#if (eq variant.slug "overload")}}
- `charge` — the drone's charge, a whole number from `0` to `OVERLOAD_AT`, as
  `specs/mode.md` states.
{{/if}}

`drones` lists every drone on the field, and a drone added through the debug surface
is appended to it.

## `BulletState` and `BurstState`

- A bullet carries an `id`, distinct among every entity live at that moment; its
  center; `vx` and `vy`, its velocity in logical units per second; `band`, the band
  it was fired with and keeps for its life; and `friendly`, `true` for one of the
  player's and `false` for an enemy bullet. One roster holds both.
- A burst carries an `id`, its center, `size`, the footprint it is played at,
  `elapsed`, the seconds it has been playing, and `sim`, its own particle
  simulation. The live particle count the debug surface reports is read from `sim`.

## The wave's own schedules

- `waveEntry` — whether the wave's own release of entry groups runs, which the debug
  surface poses.
- `diveLaunching` — whether the assault's own dive launching runs, which the debug
  surface poses.
- `entryClock` — the seconds since the wave opened, which its entry groups are
  released against. It advances only while `waveEntry` is on.
- `diveClock` — the seconds since the wave's last dive launch, which the debug
  surface poses. It advances only while `diveLaunching` is on.
- `diveTarget` — the value `diveClock` must reach for the next launch. A wave is
  built with it at `DIVE_FIRST_DELAY`, and each launch draws a fresh one between
  `DIVE_GAP_MIN` and `DIVE_GAP_MAX` multiplied by `diveGapScale(stage)`.

## The rest

- `nextId` — the id the next drone, bullet, or burst takes, so no two entities live
  at one moment share one.
- `simTime` — accumulated simulation time, in seconds. Every update adds its delta,
  whatever the screen.
- `muted` — the game's readable copy of the runtime's mute bit, refreshed in every
  update. The runtime owns muting, and this field is what the debug surface reports.
- `rngState` — the state of the game's seeded random generator. The wave's layout,
  the choice of which drone dives next, the gap before the next dive, a Flux's
  starting phase, and each burst's scatter are all drawn from it.

A drone's effective band, whether a Flux is shimmering, whether a discharge is
ready, whether the stage is a challenge stage, whether the ship is alive, and the
four stage-scaled figures are all derived by the formulas the other specs give,
rather than stored.

## The contract

The declaration above is what the debug surface reads and poses.

- Keep every field under its declared name, type, and meaning.
{{#if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
- `initialize` builds the whole state in one go, so every field is present by the
  time a frame can observe it, and none of them is optional.
- The state handed to `update` is the whole of the game, and the state `update`
  returns is the whole of the next frame. Every value the game carries from one frame
  to the next lives in it; nothing is kept in a module-level variable or a closure.
- The state is handed over read-only, as `DeepReadonly<SpectraState>`, to `update`,
  to `render`, to every diagnostic source, and to every operation of the debug
  surface. `update` and the surface's poses return a new `SpectraState` built from
  the one they received; `render`, the sources, and `snapshot` return no state.
{{else}}
- The engine builds the state when the world opens, through the mode's
  `gameStateClass`, so every field is present by the time a frame can observe it, and
  none of them is optional.
- The state is the whole of the authoritative game. Every value the game carries from
  one frame to the next lives in it; nothing is kept in a module-level variable or a
  closure, and any field an actor, component, or controller holds of its own is
  derived data rebuilt from the declared ones.
- The state is written only through the paths play runs on: the player controller
  resolves the frame's input into it, the game mode's tick advances it, and the debug
  surface's poses arrange it at the call. Rendering reads it and writes nothing.
{{/if}}
- Fields you add hold derived data you can rebuild from the declared ones. A `reset`
  restores the declared fields to their title-screen values, so those fields are the
  whole of the authoritative state.
{{/if}}
